      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">How we choose partners</p>
      <h2 class="section-title mb-12">Selection criteria</h2>
      <div class="max-w-3xl prose-content">
        <p>Technical certification is the first gate. Every partner we engage must maintain current, verifiable certifications for the technologies they represent — not just sales accreditations, but engineering-level certifications that require hands-on competency. We validate this by requiring our own engineers to achieve the same certifications independently, so that when a vendor claims a feature or a limitation, we can verify it from first-hand experience rather than a datasheet. This dual-certification model ensures that our recommendations are grounded in engineering reality, not vendor marketing.</p>
        <p>Regional presence is the second criterion. A partner must be able to support deployments across the our region — not just from a European or North American remote support desk, but with engineers who understand the local telecom landscape, the regulatory environment, and the logistics of operating in markets where a parts shipment can take three weeks to clear customs. We look for partners who have invested in regional teams, local-language support, and in-country spares depots, because these factors directly affect our ability to meet SLA commitments.</p>
        <p>Shared SLAs and operational alignment complete the picture. When a client signs an SLA with MEAICON, we need to know that our partners will stand behind their technology with the same urgency we stand behind our operations. This means co-signed escalation matrices, agreed incident response timescales, and joint participation in our incident management processes. If a vendor cannot commit to a defined escalation path and a measurable response time for P1 incidents, they are not a partner — they are a supplier, and we treat them accordingly in our architecture risk assessments.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
